WEBVTT

00:00:00.104 --> 00:00:01.523
Hi, and welcome.

00:00:01.523 --> 00:00:10.169
GitHub Enterprise Server doesn't come with Copilot code review, but many teams that run it still want an AI reviewer on every pull request.

00:00:10.169 --> 00:00:17.424
In the next few minutes I'll show you one working on a real pull request, and how you can set up the same thing on your own server.

00:00:18.228 --> 00:00:19.882
The idea is simple.

00:00:19.882 --> 00:00:27.369
When a developer opens a pull request, a GitHub Actions workflow starts a review job on a private runner.

00:00:27.369 --> 00:00:39.466
The reviewer reads the diff, asks MCP servers for context and for your own engineering rules, calls a model in Microsoft Foundry, and posts the result back as comments and a status check.

00:00:40.492 --> 00:00:40.596
Here's the lab.

00:00:40.596 --> 00:00:46.208
GHES stays in the data centre, which we simulate in Indonesia Central.

00:00:46.208 --> 00:00:56.416
A site-to-site VPN connects it to a landing zone in Southeast Asia, where the runners live on a private AKS cluster, next to two MCP servers.

00:00:56.416 --> 00:00:58.838
The runners never hold an API key.

00:00:58.838 --> 00:01:08.696
They get Entra ID tokens through workload identity and call API Management, which forwards to Foundry in East US over a private endpoint.

00:01:09.487 --> 00:01:10.724
Now the demo.

00:01:10.724 --> 00:01:16.037
A developer adds discount codes to Contoso's order service and opens a pull request.

00:01:16.037 --> 00:01:22.261
The change hides classic problems, from SQL built with an f-string to a hard-coded token.

00:01:22.261 --> 00:01:27.733
It even includes a comment telling AI reviewers that the module is already approved.

00:01:28.524 --> 00:01:32.105
A fresh runner pod on the private cluster picks up the job.

00:01:32.105 --> 00:01:37.235
It reads the diff through the GHES API, without checking out or running the code.

00:01:37.235 --> 00:01:47.522
The model first gathers context: it searches Contoso's standards for the rules behind each concern, and reads the diff through GitHub's MCP server in read-only mode.

00:01:47.522 --> 00:01:50.907
Then it reasons over everything and writes the review.

00:01:50.907 --> 00:01:54.719
The whole job took twenty-five seconds.

00:01:55.523 --> 00:01:58.075
The review lands on the pull request.

00:01:58.075 --> 00:02:07.684
One critical finding, five high and two medium, and the summary lists the Contoso standards it relied on, so the team can see at a glance what needs attention.

00:02:08.488 --> 00:02:13.202
Each finding is an inline comment on the exact line that introduced it.

00:02:13.202 --> 00:02:18.189
Here, the critical one: the discount code goes straight into an SQL string.

00:02:18.189 --> 00:02:26.563
The comment cites rule CSG-SQL-01 and offers a suggested change the developer can commit with one click.

00:02:27.406 --> 00:02:30.909
And the comment that tried to talk the reviewer into approving?

00:02:30.909 --> 00:02:33.773
It's reported as a finding, in the reviewer's own words.

00:02:33.773 --> 00:02:43.458
Everything in the pull request is treated as data, the reviewer can only comment, and Contoso's rules say to flag such text for a human.

00:02:44.249 --> 00:02:51.905
Because there are blocking findings, the AI review status fails, and branch protection keeps the merge button disabled.

00:02:51.905 --> 00:02:54.184
Notice that the unit tests passed.

00:02:54.184 --> 00:02:56.854
Tests alone didn't catch any of this.

00:02:57.671 --> 00:03:02.697
The developer pushes a fix, and the review runs again on the new commit.

00:03:02.697 --> 00:03:09.442
It caught what the fix still missed: a route that would still crash, and a redemption that was never saved.

00:03:09.442 --> 00:03:14.559
One round later it noticed the discount table shipped empty, and that the test hid it.

00:03:14.559 --> 00:03:18.677
On the fourth commit, only one low-severity note was left.

00:03:19.468 --> 00:03:21.486
Now all checks pass.

00:03:21.486 --> 00:03:31.214
The review is advisory and fast, but the decision stays with people: a teammate reads the summary, checks the remaining note, and approves the merge.

00:03:32.018 --> 00:03:35.990
Behind the scenes, API Management is the control point.

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:46.159
It validates the runner's Entra ID token, enforces a token budget for each repository, emits token metrics, and calls Foundry with its own managed identity.

00:03:46.159 --> 00:03:50.772
In our run, a review used about twenty-seven thousand tokens.

00:03:51.563 --> 00:03:57.696
To roll this out, start with a few pilot repositories and agree the data path with your security team.

00:03:57.696 --> 00:04:05.027
Build the platform: Foundry, the gateway, runners with a managed identity, and the MCP servers.

00:04:05.027 --> 00:04:08.881
Run the pilot in advisory mode and tune your rules.

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:14.755
Then make the check required, and use an organisation ruleset to cover every repository.

00:04:15.533 --> 00:04:24.244
Everything you saw is on the workshop site, with every command, output and screenshot, plus an adoption guide for your teams.

00:04:24.244 --> 00:04:30.702
And when you're done with the lab, run the cleanup script, because the gateways and the cluster are billed by the hour.

00:04:30.702 --> 00:04:32.369
Thanks for watching.
